# MOF-derived Cu embedded into N-doped mesoporous carbon as a robust support of PdAu nanocatalysts for ethanol electrooxidation

## abstract
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted widespread attention due to their large surface area and porous structure. Rationally designing the nanostructures of MOFs to promote their application in ethanol electrooxidation is still a challenge. Here, a novel Cu-NCNs (Cu-nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes) support was synthesized by pyrolysis of melamine (MEL) and Cu-ZIF-8 together, and then, Pd–Au nanoalloys were loaded by sodium borohydride reduction method to prepare PdAu@Cu-NCNs catalysts. The generating mesoporous carbon with high specific surface area and favorable electron and mass transport can be used as a potential excellent carrier for PdAu nanoparticles. In addition, the balance of catalyst composition and surface structure was tuned by controlling the content of Pd and Au. Thus, the best-performed Pd2Au2@Cu-NCN-1000–2 (where 1000 means the carrier calcination temperature, and 2 means the calcination constant temperature time) catalyst exhibits better long-term stability and electrochemical activity for ethanol oxidation in alkaline media (4.80 A·mg−1), which is 5.05 times higher than that of commercial Pd/C (0.95 A· mg−1). Therefore, this work is beneficial to further promoting the application of MOFs in direct ethanol fuel cells (DEFCs) and can be used as inspiration for the design of more efficient catalyst support structures.

Abstract Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted widespread attention due to their large surface area and porous structure. Rationally designing the nanostructures of MOFs to promote their application in ethanol electrooxidation is still a challenge. Here, a novel Cu-NCNs (Cu-nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes) support was synthesized by pyrolysis of melamine (MEL) and CuZIF-8 together, and then, Pd–Au nanoalloys were loaded by sodium borohydride reduction method to prepare PdAu@Cu-NCNs catalysts. The generating mesoporous carbon with high specific surface area and favorable electron and mass transport can be used as a potential excellent carrier for PdAu nanoparticles. In addition, the balance of catalyst composition and surface structure was tuned by controlling the content of Pd and Au. Thus, the best-performed $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{-NCN}_{-1000-2}$ (where 1000 means the carrier calcination temperature, and 2 means the calcination constant temperature time) catalyst exhibits better long-term stability and electrochemical activity for ethanol oxidation in alkaline media $(4.80~\mathrm{A{\cdot}m g^{-1}})$ , which is 5.05 times higher than that of commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}(0.95~\mathrm{A}{\cdot}~\mathrm{mg}^{-1})$ . Therefore, this work is beneficial to further promoting the application of MOFs in direct ethanol fuel cells (DEFCs) and can be used as inspiration for the design of more efficient catalyst support structures.
Keywords Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs); N-doped; Mesoporous carbon; PdAu; Ethanol electrooxidation
## 1 Introduction
In response to the increasing depletion of fossil energy such as oil, coal and natural gas, and the consequent environmental pollution and global warming problems, people have been working on the development of novel green energy conversion equipment [1–3]. Direct ethanol fuel cells (DEFCs) have received a lot of attention due to their environmental friendliness, high energy density and low cost [4]. However, the commercialization of DEFCs has two major obstacles: (1) low activity of the anode catalyst for ethanol; (2) it is easy for CO-like intermediates to poison catalyst electrodes [5]. Thus, the development of more efficient and stable DEFCs catalysts is urgent. Ptbased catalysts are the most efficient anode catalysts in ethanol oxidation. However, the high cost and low stability of $\mathrm{\Pt}$ -based catalysts seriously hinder their further development and application in alkaline DEFCs [6, 7]. Compared with Pt, Pd has a similar electronic structure, higher reserves and better resistance to CO poisoning [8–11]. This drives further exploration of more novel Pd-based catalysts for ethanol electrooxidation.
Over the past few years, binary or multi-component metal catalysts have attracted extensive attention due to their synergistic effect [12–15]. It was found that the introduction of oxyphilic metal elements (such as Au, Cu, Ni or $\mathbf{Ag})$ into binary PdM or ternary $\mathrm{PdM}_{1}\mathrm{M}_{2}$ alloys facilitated the formation of $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{-OH_{ads}}$ [16–21], which can promote the removal of toxic intermediates, thereby accelerating the ethanol oxidation reaction. The carrier is another important factor that affects the performance of the catalyst [22, 23]. At present, the most commonly used carrier for the anode catalyst of the DEFCs is carbon black, which is prone to corrosion in the harsh working environment and affects the catalytic performance [24, 25]. Therefore, selecting a suitable carrier will help to improve the catalytic activity and stability of the catalyst. The metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) composed of metal ions and organic ligands have the advantages of large specific surface area and controllable structure, which is a promising catalyst support for fuel cells [26, 27]. Among many metal–organic framework materials, the unique structure of zeolitic imidazole framework (ZIF) materials makes them ideal for preparing heteroatom- or nitrogendoped porous carbon [28, 29]. Heteroatom or nitrogen doping can effectively tune the electron density and charge polarization of the carrier material, while the porous structure helps to improve the electron and mass transport of catalysts; thus, MOFs-derived heteroatom or nitrogendoped porous carbon materials are a potentially excellent carrier material for fuel cell catalysts [30, 31].
However, there are two main problems with the abovementioned materials: First, MOFs require high-temperature pyrolysis to generate the desired carbon materials leads to a reduction in nitrogen content, which reduces catalytic activity [32]. Secondly, porous carbon is divided into macroporous carbon, mesoporous carbon and microporous carbon, and mesoporous carbon is the most favorable for catalyst electron and mass transport due to its excellent mesoporous network and mesoporous void structure [33].
To solve the above-mentioned problems, here, we prepared Cu-NCNs support by pyrolysis of melamine (MEL)
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Chemicals and materials
Copper acetylacetonate $(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{acac})_{2})$ , potassium tetrachloropalladate $(\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4})$ , hydrogen tetrachloroaurate (III) trihydrate $\mathrm{(HAuCl_{4}}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{)}$ , commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ $(10\mathrm{\wt}\%)$ and MEL were provided by Alfa Aesar. Polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP, K30), sodium borohydride $\mathrm{(NaBH_{4}}$ , $96~\mathrm{wt}\%$ ), potassium hydroxide (KOH) and Nafion solution $(5\ \mathrm{wt}\%)$ were bought from Adamas. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIM), methanol, ethanol and other chemicals were all purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co, Ltd (Shanghai, China). All the reagents were of analytical grade and used without further purification. All experiments used deionized (DI) water (resistivity $>18.0\ \mathrm{M}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm})$ .
## 2.2 Preparation of PdAu@Cu-NCNs
## 2.2.1 Preparation of Cu-ZIF-8
Cu-ZIF-8 was prepared according to the Li et al.’s work [37] with little modification. Typically, $\ensuremath{1.07~\mathrm{g}}$ Zn $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $58.9\ \mathrm{mg\Cu(acac)_{2}}$ were dissolved in 15 ml methanol at room temperature, the resulting solution was subsequently added to $10~\mathrm{ml}$ of methanol containing $1.16\mathrm{~g~}$ of 2-methyimidazole. After vigorously stirring for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , the mixed solution was kept at room temperature without stirring for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The solid products were collected by centrifugation and washed three times with methanol and dried in vacuum overnight.
## 2.2.2 Preparation of Cu-NCNs
## 2.2.3 Preparation of $P d_{x}A u_{x}@C u-N C r$ s
On the basis of Zhang et al.’s work [38], we used Cu-NCNs as the support to grow bimetallic NPs. First, a mixed solution consisting of $5\ \mathrm{ml}$ ethylene glycol and $5\mathrm{\ml}$ anhydrous ethanol was prepared, and then $25\mathrm{mgCu-NCNs}$ was put into the above solution under sonication for 30 min. Another $80~\mathrm{mg}$ of PVP was taken in a mixed solution of $10~\mathrm{ml}$ of ethylene glycol and $10~\mathrm{ml}$ of anhydrous ethanol, and ultrasonicated for $30~\mathrm{min}$ as well. Mix the above sonicated solutions together, then $20\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $20\ \mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}\ \mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4}$ were added under stirring, followed by the slow addition of KOH $(1~\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ until $\mathrm{pH}$ reached 10.0. The above solution was first stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ , and then sonicated for $30\mathrm{min}$ . After these steps, the mixture solution with $7.5~\mathrm{mg~NaBH_{4}}$ was transferred into a $50\mathrm{-ml}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and maintained at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the black product was washed with ethanol and then dried for overnight under vacuum at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The results were denoted as $\mathrm{Pd}_{1}\mathrm{Au}_{3}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{N}($ CNs, $\mathrm{Pd}_{1.5}\mathrm{Au}_{2.5}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}$ s, $\mathbf{Pd}_{2}\mathbf{Au}_{2}@\mathbf{Cu}.$ NCNs and $\mathrm{Pd}_{2.5}\mathrm{Au}_{1.5}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCNs}$ , respectively. The whole preparation process for the above catalysts was presented in Scheme 1. The loading of Pd and Au in the as-synthesized catalysts is summarized in Table S1 according to inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) analysis.
## 2.3 Characterization
X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements of the as-prepared catalysts were tested on a Bruker D8-advance. The morphology of the catalysts was investigated by field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, S-4800, Hitachi) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, FEI Tenai F20, FEI) which was equipped with an energydispersive spectroscopy (EDS). The surface functional groups of samples were analyzed by the Thermo Electron Group Nicolet 6700 Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectrometer in a frequency range of $400{\-}4000\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and using KBr as a diluent. The elemental composition of the synthesized materials was characterized by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Escalab250XI, Thermo Scientific). ICP-OES (ICAP 7000, Thermo Scientific) was used to determine the accurate content of Pd and Au. The effect of different calcination temperatures on the support was investigated using a thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA, STA-449, Germany). The specific surface area and pore size distribution of the catalysts were tested by Brunner–Emmet–Teller measurements (BET, HD88, USA) and Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) method, respectively.
## 2.4 Electrochemical experiment
The catalytic performance of the catalysts was evaluated in a standard three-electrode system using a CHI 760E electrochemical workstation. The saturated calomel electrode (SCE) was used as the reference electrode. A glassy carbon electrode (GC, $3\ \mathrm{mm}$ inner diameter) and the platinum piece $(1\mathrm{cm}\times1\mathrm{cm})$ were employed as the working and counter electrodes, respectively. Before testing, the glassy carbon electrodes were polished with 0.3 and $0.05~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ alumina powders, and then sonicated alternately with deionized water and anhydrous ethanol for $5~\mathrm{min}$ . Catalyst inks were fabricated according to the following procedure: adding $2.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalysts to a mixture contained $700~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ isopropyl alcohol and $290~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ deionized water with ultrasonic treatment for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , then $10~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ of Nafion solution $(5\mathrm{\wt\%})$ was added and sonicated again for $30~\mathrm{min}$ Afterward, $2.0~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ catalyst ink was dripped onto glassy carbon surface and dried at room temperature. For electrochemical measurements, all solutions of electrolyte were saturated with high-purity ${\bf N}_{2}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Impedance measurements of catalysts were made with a $5\mathrm{-mV}$ sinusoid superimposed on a direct current (DC) potential in 1 mo $\mathrm{1{\cdot}L^{-1}\ K O H}+1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ ethanol at peak potential vs. SCE with a frequency range of $0.1~\mathrm{Hz}$ to $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ A Randles equivalent circuit was used to obtain the values of circuit components.
## 3 Results and discussion
Figure S5 displays SEM images of the best-performed $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}-1000-2$ composite catalyst. According to Fig. $S5\mathrm{a-c}$ , due to the introduction of melamine as an additional nitrogen source, PdAu NPs are uniformly distributed on the porous Cu-NCN-1000–2 [41]. Additionally, the EDS results also show that the elements Pd and Au are well dispersed on the Cu-NCN-1000–2 which can lead to the increase in the electrocatalyst activity of the $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-1000-2}.$ Figures S6 and S8 show SEM images of $\mathrm{PdAu}@\mathrm{Cu}–\mathrm{NCNs}$ with different temperatures, pyrolysis time and the ratios of Pd to Au. As shown in Figs. S6 and S7, when the calcination temperature is too low or the calcination time is too short, the PdAu NPs will have obvious agglomeration on the surface of catalysts, and when the calcination temperature is too high or the calcination time is too long, the number of PdAu NPs will be significantly reduced, which is not conducive to improving the electrochemical performance of the electrocatalytic activity. To sum up, we can conclude that the optimal calcination temperature is $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the optimal calcination time is $2\mathrm{~h~}$ .
The morphologies and composition of the best-performed $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ catalyst were investigated using transmission electron microscope (TEM). The TEM images and corresponding size distribution histograms illustrate the distribution of PdAu NPs and lattice structure. According to Fig. 1a, b, d, the PdAu nanoparticles with the mean size of $10.0\mathrm{nm}$ are uniformly dispersed on the surface of the support. This may be the mesoporous carbon support formed by introducing melamine as an additional nitrogen source which can effectively prevent agglomeration and offer anchoring sites for PdAu nanoparticles [41]. In Fig. 1c, a series of lattice spacings of
$0.22{\mathrm{~nm}}$ were observed, just between that of fcc-structured Pd $(0.23~\mathrm{nm})$ and Au $(0.21~\mathrm{nm})$ , which can prove the formation of the $\mathtt{P d A u C u}$ alloy [42, 43]. This is also well consistent with the XRD results. EDX mapping results (Fig. 1e–i) showed the uniformed distribution of Pd, Au, Cu, N and C elements in $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}$ -NCN-1000–2 catalyst, respectively.
Additionally, we conducted XRD analyses to verify the structural features of our synthesized materials. In Fig. S9, the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -ZIF-8 is in line with previous literature [40], proving the successful preparation of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -ZIF-8. Besides, both precursors have similar XRD pattern, indicating that $\mathtt{C u}$ -ZIF-8/MEL maintains the CuZIF-8’s characteristic structure. As shown in Fig. S10, as a result of the formation of graphitic structure and amorphous carbon during pyrolysis, all the Cu-NCNs supports display two broad peaks at around $23^{\circ}$ and $44^{\circ}$ , respectively. These broads correspond to carbon (002) and (101) diffractions. Besides, the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Cu}.$ - NCN-900–2, Cu-NCN-1000–1, Cu-NCN-1000–4 and CuNCN-1000–6 correspond to (111), (200) and (220) planes of Cu detected at around $43.4^{\circ}$ , $50.4^{\circ}$ and $74.3^{\circ}$ , respectively, which indicate the existence of pure copper [40]. This further proves that suitable temperature and calcination time are beneficial to generate uniformly distributed Cu-NCNs supports, which are beneficial to improve electrochemical activity and stability and also consistent with the SEM results. At the same time, we further proved the successful doping of PdAu nanoparticles by XRD test. According to Figs. 2a and S10b, d, typical broad diffraction peaks were observed at $38.4^{\circ}$ , $44.5^{\circ}$ , $64.8^{\circ}$ and $77.8^{\circ}$ for all catalysts, which are correlated with the (111), (200), (220) and (311) planes of fcc metallic Au [44]. In addition, it can be seen that the diffraction peak of the catalyst not only increases gradually with the increase in temperature, reaching the lowest at $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , but also decreases with the increase in pyrolysis time, and reaches the highest when the pyrolysis time is $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . Upon adding Pd to Au, the peaks of $\mathrm{Pd}_{x}\mathrm{Au}_{4}$ - $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{x}@\mathbf{Cu-NCN-1000-2}$ shift gradually to higher $2\theta$ values, because Pd is embedded into the lattice of Au, causing the lattice shrinkage of Au. At the same time, all $\mathrm{Pd}_{x}\mathrm{Au}_{4}.$ - $\mathbf{\Lambda}_{x}@\mathbf{Cu-NCN-1000-}2$ catalysts have lost the (111) peak of Pd. This further demonstrates the successful synthesis of PdAu alloy on Cu-NCNs supports.
The FTIR spectra of the optimal performed Cu-NCN1000–2 and $\mathbf{Pd}_{2}\mathbf{Au}_{2}@\mathbf{Cu}.$ -NCNs are shown in Fig. 2b. It is observed that when PdAu NPs is doped, the characteristic peaks of the catalyst are almost the same as those of the support. The peak near $1072~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is $\scriptstyle{\mathbf{C}}-{\mathbf{O}}$ skeleton vibration, the peak near $1640~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ skeleton vibration, the peak at $3432~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is caused by the stretching vibration of $\mathrm{O-H}$ . This indicates that the synthesized $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-}1000{-}2$ still retains the characteristic functional groups of Cu-NCNs-1000–2.
We used TGA to further explore the change of catalyst precursor with increase in calcination temperature. As shown in Fig. S11, the $\mathrm{Cu}$ -ZIF-8 and $\mathtt{C u}$ -ZIF-8/MEL began to show a significant decline curve after $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , indicating that the pyrolysis of ZIF-8 started from $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In addition, $\mathrm{Cu-ZIF-8/MEL}$ has a distinct drop process between 300 and $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is here due to the pyrolysis of the doped melamine. Finally, it can be observed that when the calcination temperature reaches $1100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the mass loss of CuZIF-8 is $60.9~\mathrm{wt}\%$ , while the mass loss of Cu-ZIF-8/MEL formed by doping $\mathrm{Cu}$ -ZIF-8 with melamine at a mass ratio of 1:1 is $70.0\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ , which also proves that the introduction of melamine can improve the stability of the catalyst.
Figure 2c, d shows the ${\bf N}_{2}$ absorption–desorption isotherms and the related pore size distribution curves. As shown in Fig. 2c, Cu-NCN-1000–2 support has type IV isotherms with H1 hysteresis loops, indicting a typical mesoporous structure [45, 46]. In addition, Cu-NCN-1000–2 has a specific surface area as high as $1266~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a pore volume of $0.91~\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 2d, Cu-NCN1000–2 as a carrier showed distinct stratification pore structure with both micropores $(1-2~\mathrm{nm})$ , mesopores (2–50 nm) and macropores $(>50~\mathrm{nm})$ ), which is related to the pore size distribution of its precursor. By introducing melamine, the pore size can be increased, which facilitates electron and electrolyte transportation [47–49]. It can be seen that CuNCN-1000–2 is an excellent mesoporous material that improves electron transport and quality, and at the same time, improves the performance of catalysts [50].
In order to determine the optimal pyrolysis temperature and time, we tested the ethanol electrooxidation performance of the catalysts prepared under different conditions. As shown in Fig. S14, the $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}-1000-2$ catalyst shows the highest mass activity of $4.8\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}$ , higher than that of $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{-NCN}{\mathrm{-}}800{\mathrm{-}}2$ $(3.7~\mathrm{A{\cdot}m g^{-1}})$ , $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}$ -NCN-900–2 $(2.0~\mathrm{A{\cdot}m g^{-1}},$ ), $\mathbf{Pd}_{2}\mathbf{Au}_{2}@\mathbf{Cu}-$ NCN-1100–2 (2.9 $\mathbf{A\cdotmg}^{-1}$ ), $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-1000-1}$ $(0.7~\mathrm{A{\cdot}m g^{-1}})$ ), $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{-NCN}\mathrm{-}1000{-}4}&{{}(1.2\mathrm{~A}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}^{-1})}\end{array}$ and $\mathrm{Pd_{2}A u_{2}@C u-N C N-1000-6_{\Delta\left(0.9\ A\cdot m g^{-1}\right)}}$ . In addition to these results, we found that $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ were the ideal pyrolysis temperature and time, which is also consistent with the results of XRD and SEM. In addition, it indicated that this novel carbon support could serve as an excellent catalyst support for DEFCs when further nitrogen is added and the pyrolysis temperature and time are adjusted.
Figure 3a indicates cyclic voltammetry (CV) of $\mathrm{Pd}_{x}\mathrm{Au}_{y}/$ Cu-NCN-1000–2 and commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ catalyst in 1 mol $\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ KOH solution. Typical, all catalysts have one reduction peak in the backward scan (from $-0.5$ to $-0.2$ V) and two oxidation peaks in the forward scan (from $-0.7$ to $-0.4\mathrm{~V~}$ and $-0.3$ to $0\mathrm{~V~}$ ). Based on the different electrochemical processes occurring on nanocatalysts surfaces, the first oxidation peak corresponded to the electrochemical adsorption of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ on the Pd-based catalysts to form $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-OH_{ads}}}$ , while the second oxidation peak was assigned to the further oxidation of $\mathrm{Pd\mathrm{-OH_{ads}}}$ with another $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ into Pd–O. However, the reduction peaks were ascribed to the electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{Pd-O}$ into metallic Pd [43], which could be used to calculate the electrochemically surface area (ECSA). The ECSA of $\mathbf{Pd}_{1}\mathbf{Au}_{3}@\mathbf{Cu}.$ -NCN1000–2, $\mathrm{Pd_{1.5}A u_{2.5}}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-1000-2},$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{Pd}_{2}\mathbf{Au}_{2}@\mathbf{Cu}.}\end{array}$ -NCN1000–2, $\mathrm{Pd}_{2.5}\mathrm{Au}_{1.5}@\mathrm{Cu}$ -NCN-1000–2 and commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/$ C are 47.4, 34.3, 41.2, 21.7 and $38.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Figure 3b shows the stabilized CV curves of the EOR (ethanol oxidation reaction) on $\mathrm{Pd_{\itx}A u_{\it y}/C u-N C N-1000-2}$ catalysts from $-0.8$ to $0.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. SCE at the scan rate of 50 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in $\mathrm{1~\mol{\cdot}L^{-1}~\ K O H+1~\ m o l{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ ethanol. According to Fig. 3b, with the increase in the $\mathrm{Pd/Au}$ molar ratio, the corresponding peak potential is also shifted positively, which indicates that adding Au element may delay the catalyst’s peak potential. In addition, two distinct peaks can be seen, and the peak in the forwarding scan could be related the oxidation of ethanol, while the peak in the reverse scan is assigned to the further removal of the intermediates. The peak current density increases and then decreases as the Pd/Au molar ratio increases from 1:3,
1.5:2.5 and 2:2 to 2.5:1.5. As shown in Fig. 3b, the $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{-NCN}\mathrm{-}1000{-}2$ catalyst displays the highest mass activity of $4.8~\mathrm{A{\cdot}m g^{-1}}$ , which is 2.2, 1.9, 2.5 and 4.8 times than that of $\mathrm{Pd}_{1}\mathrm{Au}_{3}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ (2.2 $\mathbf{A\cdotmg}^{-1},$ ), $\mathrm{Pd}_{1.5}\mathrm{Au}_{2.5}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}-1000-2$ (2.5 $\mathbf{A\cdotmg^{-1}},$ ), $\mathrm{Pd}_{2.5}\mathrm{Au}_{1.5}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ ( $\mathrm{{(1.9\A{\cdot}m g^{-1}}}$ ) and commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ $(1.0~\mathrm{A{\cdot}m g^{-1}},$ ), respectively.
Table 1 shows the ethanol oxidation initiation potentials, peak potentials and corresponding peak mass activities of $\mathrm{Pd}_{x}\mathrm{Au}_{y}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ catalysts and commercial $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ catalysts. From Table 1, we can also conclude that $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-}1000-2$ catalyst also has the most negative onset potential as well as the most positive peak potential compared to other $\mathrm{Pd}_{x}\mathrm{Au}_{y}@\mathrm{Cu}$ -NCN-1000–2 catalysts and commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ catalysts. This may be because the introduction of Au is beneficial to reduce the surface energy and stabilize the lattice structure, and delay the oxidation potential to a more positive value, thus ensuring that there are still more EOR active sites at high potentials [44].
Chronoamperometry (CA) was carried out to further explore the electrocatalytic stability of the as-synthesized $\mathrm{Pd}_{x}\mathrm{Au}_{y}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ at $-0.25\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig. 3c). The CA curves show that the $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ exhibits much higher mass activity than other $\mathrm{Pd}_{x}\mathrm{Au}_{y}@\mathrm{Cu}$ -NCNs1000–2 and commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ at all time, further demonstrating that the $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{-NCN}\mathrm{-}1000{-}2$ has a significantly improved electrocatalytic for EOR. In addition, Fig. 3c, d also shows that $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ owns a slower current decay over time in comparison with the other $\mathrm{Pd}_{x}\mathrm{Au}_{y}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ and commercial $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ . For example, the mass activity of $\mathbf{Pd}_{2}\mathbf{Au}_{2}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}-$ NCN
1000–2 after 1800s is $59.7\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pd}}{}^{-1}$ , which is higher than that of $\mathrm{Pd}_{1}\mathrm{Au}_{3}@\mathrm{Cu}$ -NCN-1000–2 $(11.2\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pd}}^{-1})$ , $\mathrm{Pd_{1.5}A u_{2.5}}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-1000-2}$ (30.0 $\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}m g_{P d}}^{-1})$ , $\mathrm{Pd}_{2.5}\mathrm{Au}_{1.5}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ $(18.9\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pd}}^{-1}$ ) and commercial Pd/C $(29.3\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{Pd}}^{-1})$ , indicating that $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-}1000-2$ has much higher tolerance to carbonaceous species generated during the oxidation of ethanol.
As illustrated in Fig. 4a, b, the CV curves of commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-}1000-2$ at different scanning rates show that as the scan rate gradually increases, the mass activity of the catalysts also increases gradually, and the peak potential shift occurs. At the same time, it can be observed from the insets of Fig. 4a, b that there is an obvious linear relationship between the corresponding forward peak current density and the square root of the scanning rate; besides, the slope of $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu-NCN-}1000-2$ catalyst is 0.260, which is much larger than that of commercial Pd/C. These all demonstrate that $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ catalyst has significantly improved methanol electrooxidation kinetics.
We evaluated the electrochemical durability performance of $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}-1000-2$ catalyst by accelerated durability testing. As shown in Fig. 4c, d, after 300 cycles of CV tests in $1\ \mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ methanol $+\mathrm{~1~}\mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ KOH electrolyte, the ratio of residual current density to initial current density of $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCN}_{-1000-2}$ catalyst is $49.2\%$ , which is significantly better than that of commercial Pd/C $(29.7\%)$ . This result further shows that the $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{-NCN}_{-1000-2}$ catalyst has an excellent electrochemical durability.
To further clarify the promoting effect of activation on the electrocatalytic activity toward the EOR, Fig. S15 shows the Nyquist plots of $\mathrm{Pd_{1}A u_{3}@C u-N C N s-1000-2},$ $\mathrm{Pd_{1.5}A u_{2.5}@C u-N C N s\ –1000-2.}$ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Pd}_{2}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Au}_{2}}\ensuremath{\circledcirc}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Cu}}$ -NCNs1000–2, $\mathrm{Pd}_{2.5}\mathrm{Au}_{1.5}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCNs}\ –1000-2$ and commercial $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ catalysts in $1\ \mathrm{mol{\cdot}L^{-1}\ K O H+1\ m o l{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ ethanol solution at peak potential. The (electrochemical impedance spectroscopy) EIS results indicate that the ethanol electrooxidation on all as-synthesized catalysts has different impedance. The semicircle diameter represents the resistance to the charge transfer; the faster the charge transfer rate, the smaller the diameter [57]. The diameter of impedance arc (DIA) of catalysts from the minimum to maxim is: commercial $\mathrm{Pd/C}<\mathrm{Pd}_{2.5}\mathrm{Au}_{1.5}@\mathrm{Cu}$ -NCNs$1000-2<\mathrm{Pd_{1}A u_{3}}@\mathrm{Cu-NCNs-1000-2}<\mathrm{Pd_{1.5}A u_{2}}$ .5@CuNCNs- $1000{-}2<\operatorname{Pd}_{2}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{u}_{2}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ -NCNs-1000–2. Notably, the $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{Au}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}-\mathrm{NCNs}\cdot1000-2$ had the smallest DIA among all the catalysts, implying that it has higher satisfactory charge transfer ability and electrochemical conductivity in the ethanol solution, and also has a positive effect on improving electrocatalytic performance. These results are in agreement with the results obtained from CV and CA methods.
